<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Milestone 1 entitlements. Three, and only three.

	  Deliberately ABSENT: network client, network server, iCloud, CloudKit, Contacts,
	  Reminders, Photos, Camera, Microphone, Location, Bluetooth, USB, Downloads folder, Apple Events,
	  JIT, and every temporary exception.

	  Without the network-client entitlement the sandbox refuses outbound connections at the kernel
	  boundary, which is what makes "this app makes no network requests" verifiable rather than a
	  promise. An entitlement is added in the same commit as the feature that needs it and no earlier.
	  See docs/06-privacy-and-entitlements.md.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Open and save panels: import, export, and attaching files. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Security-scoped bookmarks for files the user keeps outside the app. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  Reading and writing the calendar.

	  This entitlement lets the sandbox reach CalendarAgent at all. The TCC prompt is separate and is
	  triggered only when the user turns the feature on in Settings — until then the app holds an
	  inert provider and never touches EventKit.

	  EventKit has no read-only tier: its only requests are full access and write-only, so any app
	  that reads must ask for full access. Phase D used only the read half and proved it with a test
	  asserting that no mutating call existed anywhere. The calendar module uses both halves, so that
	  test was replaced rather than deleted, by three narrower properties `CalendarWriteSafetyTests`
	  still enforces: writes take an `EventDraft` and nothing else, so the CRM has nowhere to leak
	  through; mutating EventKit calls exist in exactly three named methods; and nothing is saved or
	  deleted across a series without an explicit scope.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  The People module: reading the address book.

	  This entitlement lets the sandbox reach the Contacts daemon at all. The TCC prompt is separate
	  and is triggered only when the user turns the integration on in Settings — until then the app
	  holds an inert `NoContactsProvider` and never touches `CNContactStore`.

	  Contacts, unlike EventKit, has a single access tier: granting read access grants write access
	  with it, and the user has no way to give less. Elephruit never writes. `ContactsProviding` has
	  no write method, and `ContactsWriteSafetyTests` fails if the adapter ever reaches past it to
	  `CNSaveRequest` — and a second test fails if the adapter fetches a key nothing displays.

	  Still deliberately ABSENT: network client, network server, iCloud, CloudKit, Reminders, Photos,
	  Camera, Microphone, Location, Bluetooth, USB, Apple Events, JIT, and every temporary exception.
	  Business-card scanning needs no camera entitlement: Continuity Camera is presented by the
	  system, which hands back a file, and Vision reads it on-device.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
